# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil

The queue, in priority order, top first. Each entry states the fact,
the fix and the check that settles it. Finished entries leave; git
keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the longer
investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* TODO The wide half of UserCtype

=tests/src/ctype.cpp= implements =UserCtype<wchar_t>= and
=UserCtype<UserChar>= in part: ten members are "FIXME: implement
this" stubs. =make_char= (=tests/include/rw_char.h=) takes only a
=char=. =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp= stops its table at =UCHAR_MAX=, so
the wide entries have never run.

Fix: finish the helper, give =make_char= an =int= overload, remove the
break.

Check: =0.ctype= exercises the whole table for all three character
types.

* TODO README section 5 and the configuration

The original README says to set =CPPOPTS= and =CXXOPTS= on the make
command line to change compiler options. The configuration does not
see them, by design. =config.h= is a function of the compiler, its
version and the build type; what the configuration must see goes in
the =CONFIG= file. A dialect option given only on the command line
configures one language and builds the library in another.

Fix: prose, no patch. The per-platform manual step the README
describes is a defect to move away from. =README.md= says so; the
original README stays as it is.

Check: none; this is prose.

* TODO MT tests that provoke contention instead of waiting for it

The older =22.locale.*.mt= tests run one thread per processor, up to
32, for 10,000 to 200,000 iterations each. Each threaded section runs
to its own 60-second soft timeout. After the first iteration
everything they touch is cached, and the rest measures the locks at
rest. On 32 processors they also measure the harness: its 1 GiB
address-space limit (=etc/config/GNUmakefile.tst=) cannot hold 32
threads' stacks and =malloc= arenas, and a test that starts outlasts
the harness's 60-second timeout. Each test covers several facets, so
a failure names a test, not a defect.

The four gated tests, =22.locale.use_facet.mt=,
=22.locale.time.put.libc.mt=, =22.locale.id.mt= and
=22.locale.facet.mt=, find their races in seconds. A few threads wait
on a barrier and start together at the first use of a fresh object,
round after round. They overlap the older tests.

Fix: rewrite the older tests in that shape, or retire those the gated
tests cover, one race or one facet operation per program: 4 to 8
threads whatever the processor count, a barrier, many short rounds on
fresh state, a small thread stack. Steady-state races are left to the
ThreadSanitizer build. With a small fixed thread count the 1 GiB
limit stays a guard and the timeout becomes a hang detector.

Check: a census of what each older test covers against the gated
ones, each kept, split or retired with the reason. Every locale MT
test passes in seconds under the harness in 15D on any processor
count, and the 15D rows become a reference. Each test still fails
against the library before the fix it covers.

* TODO One atomic backend, the =__atomic= built-ins

The atomic operations have three backends besides the mutex
templates: the GNU =__sync= built-ins (=<rw/_atomic-sync.h>=), x86
assembly (=<rw/_atomic-x86.h>=, =src/x86/atomic.s=) and x86-64
assembly (=<rw/_atomic-x64.h>=, =src/x86_64/atomic.s=). The =__sync=
branch serves every configuration of the matrix, 32-bit x86 included.
The assembly runs only when the =int= characterization fails. =__sync=
is a full barrier for the arithmetic and an acquire barrier only for
=__sync_lock_test_and_set=, which =_RWSTD_ATOMIC_SWAP= inherits; x86
hides the difference behind =xchg=. The library exports the assembly
routines, =__rw_atomic_add= and =__rw_atomic_xchg= at 8, 16, 32 and
(x86-64) 64 bits. 32-bit binaries built before a976e3b9 call them.

Fix: one backend over the =__atomic= built-ins with explicit orders,
none weaker than today: =__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST= for the increments, the
decrements and the exchange. Weakening an order is the next entry's
business, site by site. The five characterizations test the
=__atomic= operations the backend uses; their macros stay.
=<rw/_atomic-x86.h>=, =<rw/_atomic-x64.h>= and their dispatch branches
go. =src/atomic.s= and the two assembly sources stay built and
exported until the next minor version, which may break the binary
interface. Then they go with their symbols.

Check: the x86-64 and aarch64 suites unchanged against their tables.
The disassembly shows =lock xadd= and =xchg= on x86-64 and 32-bit x86,
and =ldaddal= and =swpal= behind the outline helpers on aarch64. The
library's exported symbols unchanged.

* TODO Synchronization on atomic integer flags: a sweep

Some one-time initializations elect their initializer with an atomic
increment. They publish completion with a plain write to a =volatile=
flag the other threads spin on. =volatile= orders neither the flag
against the data it guards nor the spin against the reads after it.
Known sites:
- =src/locale_body.cpp:807-830=, the global locale. The builder
  stores =global= at 817 with no release, then =ginit += 1000= as a
  plain read-modify-write. Readers wait on =ginit < 1000= with no
  acquire and read =global= at 830; on aarch64 they can read 0.
  =global= is also tested unlocked at 807, against the store under
  the lock at 841: the one ThreadSanitizer report. The election
  exists because =_C_manage (0, "C")= takes the same static guard,
  and the mutex is not recursive.
- =include/rw/_mutex.h:286=, the static mutex, with a non-atomic
  fallback where atomics are absent.
- =src/once.cpp:125= and =src/iostream.cpp:247=.
The list is a start, not a census.

Fix: census every site that synchronizes on an atomic or =volatile=
integer. Decide each: a release store and an acquire load through the
compiler built-ins, the site's mutex, or =__rw_once=.

Check: the census in a note, each site with its decision. The
ThreadSanitizer build reports none of them, and three rounds of every
=22.locale.*.mt= test in it report nothing from the library.

* TODO =yi_US.CP1255= does not build on aarch64

=make locales= in a 15D build on aarch64 Linux stops at =yi_US.CP1255=.
=localedef= reports Error 308, "incomplete multibyte character in
character map file: expecting 4 bytes, found 0". Nothing records the
locales phase on x86-64, so whether it fails only on aarch64 is open.
Hypothesis: =char= is unsigned on aarch64 and signed on x86, and the
charmap reader depends on the sign of a byte.

Fix: to be found.

Check: =make locales= builds every locale in =etc/nls/gen_list= on
aarch64, and the databases match the x86-64 ones byte for byte.

* TODO Keep instantiated facets until the program ends

A named facet dies with the last locale that holds it. The next locale
of that name builds it again: the constructor, the mapping of its
database, the initialization of its data. The locale MT tests that
build locales in a loop pay for it. 15D on aarch64, lock-free atomics,
5-run means of real time, before and after 2bbdf7c9 stopped the
=use_facet= leak that had kept facets alive by accident:
=22.locale.ctype.mt= 11.9 s to 13.0 s, =22.locale.numpunct.mt= 15.1 s
to 16.7 s, =22.locale.globals.mt= 8.3 s to 9.3 s.

Fix: explore a facet repository that keeps every facet it
instantiates until the program ends: its cost in memory and mappings,
what it changes in the reference counts and =~__rw_locale=, and what
it does to the lifetime of the facet data.

Check: designed with the fix. The timings above are the baseline, and
=22.locale.use_facet.mt= still finds no block outliving its round, or
leaves with the design.

* TODO The C++17 floor

The library is C++98 and characterizes the compiler. The intended
step raises the implementation floor to C++17: =nullptr=, =noexcept=
in place of the dynamic exception specifications, =static_assert=,
=override=, inline variables for the facet statics, =<type_traits>=
for the hand-rolled traits, and the characterizations for compilers
that no longer exist retired. This is not the conformance target,
what the library gives its users; that is a roadmap of its own.

The =0= to =nullptr= sweep is one inert commit on top of the floor
change, never ahead of it. Until then null is the literal =0=.

* TODO Strict ANSI as the rule, the additions outside the standard gone

=_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= (=include/rw/_config.h:234=, also selected by
=_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS=) is opt-in. It undefines =_RWSTD_LONG_LONG= and
defines 30 =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= macros. The original README calls the
switch deprecated. The tree reads 38 =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= names in 40
files under =include= and =src=; the strict block never sets eight of
them. The tests carry the same gates, and =tests/intro/17.extensions=
exercises the additions.

Intent: strict conformance becomes the only configuration. What
leaves is what the library adds beyond ISO/IEC 14882:2003: members,
classes, overloads and manipulator values the standard does not
specify. Among them the =ios_base::failure= subclasses, base-2 stream
I/O and =ios_base::bin=, =stringbuf::str (const char*)=, the
deep-copy =basic_string::copy=, the extra =num_get=, =num_put= and
=time_get= members, the =void= forms of =count= and =distance=, the
=setbase= bases beyond the required ones, the filebuf and
stream-locking additions.

The rest of what the gates cover keeps its behaviour and loses only
its macro: choices the standard permits (the in-place =assign= and
=insert= of =deque= and =vector=, the width kept on a failed
insertion, the portable random sequence, the primary-template
definitions), later defect resolutions the block disables under the
extension name (LWG 559, cv-qualified =numeric_limits=; LWG 568, the
safe stream conversion), and =long long=, which follows the C++17
floor.

Fix, one subsystem per commit, the suite tables as the oracle. First,
the 38 gates sorted into the two lists, recorded in the commit that
deletes =_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= and =_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS= from
=_config.h=. Then, per addition, the code path goes with its macro,
its test branches and its =17.extensions= cases; per kept behaviour,
the macro and its dead branch go. The original README stays;
=README.md= records the policy. The order against the C++17 floor is
decided before the first commit.

Check: =git grep _RWSTD_NO_EXT_= is empty under =include=, =src= and
=tests=. =17.extensions= is reduced to nothing or retired. The suite
tables are re-pinned in all six configurations with no row moving but
the extension rows. The examples build.

* TODO Leftovers beside the removed function probes

The removal of the C library function probes (f43be017..a44256a6)
kept to the code that read the probe macros. Its census found dead
or stale code beside them. No tracked configuration compiles any of
it differently.

- =_RWSTD_NO_USING_LIBC_IN_STD= is defined nowhere. Fifteen headers
  test it around their =namespace std= blocks.
- =src/file.cpp= declares =mkstemp= and =fileno= under
  =#ifndef _RWSTD_NO_PURE_C_HEADERS=, which the file defines three
  lines above; the blocks cannot be compiled.
- =_cstdio.h= declares =gets= in =std= and =stdio.h= exports it, both
  in pure-headers mode only. The default mode leaves it out, as
  C++14 does.
- =_RWSTD_FMT_TYPE=, =_RWSTD_FMT_ASSGN=, =_STRTOLL= and =_STRTOULL=
  in =_defs.h= have had no user since the 2005 import.
  =_RWSTD_WCSFTIME_ARG3_T= has no reader outside its
  characterization. =config.h= carries =_RWSTD_NO_ABS_INT=; nothing
  reads it.
- =<cmath>='s =_FABSF= calls =fabsf= without the =::= of its 43
  siblings. The 44 =_<F>F= and =_<F>L= macros could be inlined into
  the overloads that call them.
- =21.cwchar= exercises none of =wcscoll=, =wcsncat=, =vswprintf=,
  =vwprintf=, =getwchar=, =putwchar=.
- =libc_decl.sh= only looks for headers now; its name says more.
  =headers.inc= holds one list, for one script.
- Hypothesis: =__rw_get_timepunct= calls =wcsftime= with wide literal
  formats whatever =_RWSTD_NO_WCSFTIME_WCHAR_T_FMAT= says; on a C
  library whose third parameter is =const char*= it would not
  compile. Check: =git log -L= on those calls for a platform note.

Fix: one commit per item, each inert on the six configurations
except the =21.cwchar= coverage, which adds assertions.

Check: the per-commit check of the removal (=config.h=, object code,
the non-debug recompile, the suite table) in =11S=.

* TODO A missing brace in a =replace= call formatter

The case =replace_iter_iter_size_val= of the call formatter
(=tests/src/21.strings.cpp=) spells its second conditional
=%{? + %zu%{;}=, without the brace that closes =%{?}=. Each argument
after it is printed one place early. Row 645 of =21.string.replace=
passes a count of 1 and ='c'=; its diagnostics read
=replace (begin(), begin(), 0, '\x01')=. The calls and the assertions
are right; only the text is wrong. The defect dates from 2006.

Fix: =%{?} + %zu%{;}=.

Check: under =--trace=, the rows of that overload print the count and
the character they pass. The suite table does not move.

* TODO The next minor version

The version rule: the first number is source compatibility, the
second binary compatibility. Three changes break the binary interface
of 4.2.x and wait for the next minor version:
- The string reference count. Thread-safe builds on Linux/x86-64 keep
  a mutex in every string body (=_RWSTD_NO_STRING_ATOMIC_OPS=,
  =include/rw/_config.h:101-114=). The clause cites 4.1.x; its effect
  is the string layout of every 4.2.x release. =21.string.stdcxx-162=
  aborts on it in 15D: the body is 64 bytes, the test allows 24. The
  mutex is correct, only larger and slower.
  =_RWSTD_USE_STRING_ATOMIC_OPS= selects the atomics today.
- The =numpunct= cache members, unused since 2e196ebb
  (=include/loc/_numpunct.h:117=).
- =src/x86/atomic.s=, =src/x86_64/atomic.s= and the symbols they
  export, once the =__atomic= backend entry lands.

Fix: when the version moves, all three go: delete the string clause
rather than define the opt-in, remove the members, remove the
sources and their symbols.

Check: =21.string.stdcxx-162= passes in 15D. The exported symbols
lose exactly the assembly routines. The suites are otherwise
unchanged.

* Documentation

Documents that describe the library as it is, for a reader, not for
the record. They go under =doc/notes= with the =ref-= prefix. Each
entry names the document, what it must answer and where the material
is. They are prose: no check beyond a reading. Roughly in order of
payback.

** TODO A tour of the locale subsystem

Written: =doc/notes/ref-locale.md=, current as of 2026-09-27. The
entry leaves after a reading.

Facets and their ids, the facet index scheme, =locale_body=, how
=combine= and the global locale share bodies, the byname facets and
the binary locale data they read. A reader of
=doc/notes/analysis-locale-mt-race.md= needs this map first. Shape:
=ref-string-replace.md=.

** TODO Decision records

One short file per irreversible choice of the revival: the platform
retirement, the null-pointer literal, the commit shape, the Clang
configuration, the compatibility mutex in the string body. The tree
has the outcomes; the reasons are in commit messages and private
notes. Material: the commit messages and
=doc/notes/plan-platform-state.md=.

** TODO A conformance record

Written: =doc/notes/ref-conformance.md=, current as of 2026-09-27.
The entry leaves after a reading.

What of ISO/IEC 14882:2003 the library implements, where it deviates
knowingly, which LWG issues it applies (the headers cite them by
number), and which later library features it lacks. The roadmap the
C++17 floor entry defers to. The most useful to an outside reader,
and the most work.

** TODO A build-system reference

How the =BUILDTYPE= digits and letters map to flags, why =config.h=
is a function of the compiler and =makefile.in= only, how a
characterization is written and how its result gates code, the serial
phases. The original =README= describes 2008; the revival's rules are
spread over =README.md= and the commit messages.

** TODO The characterization catalogue

Written: =doc/notes/ref-characterizations.md=, current as of
2026-09-27. The entry leaves after a reading.

The probes under =etc/config/src=, each yielding a =_RWSTD_NO_*=
macro: a table of macro, what it detects, which supported compiler
trips it today, and whether it is still reachable. It shows what the
C++17 floor could retire.

** TODO An ABI and versioning note

What the shared library exports, how the version macros and the
=_RWSTD_VER= checks work, what a change to the string body would
break. The entry on the next minor version lists the changes that
wait for it.

** TODO A test-suite guide

How the driver's =rw_assert= and =rw_test= work, the =TEST= table
idiom and its =bthrow= column, how the harness classifies rows, how
to write a regression against a table test. Fragments are in
=doc/notes/ref-test-baseline.md=.

** TODO A threading model note

Written: =doc/notes/ref-threading.md=, current as of 2026-09-27. The
entry leaves after a reading.

What the library promises under =_RWSTD_REENTRANT=, what the string's
reference counting protects and what it does not, the facet caches
ThreadSanitizer named. The front matter for the MT entries.

** TODO A tour of the streams

The =basic_streambuf= buffering contract, the =ios_base= storage, how
=filebuf= maps onto the C library. Shape: =ref-string-replace.md=.
